## 3. AWS SERVICES USED

### 3.1 Amazon Elastic Container Service (ECS) with Fargate

**Purpose and Implementation:**

Amazon ECS with Fargate serves as the application hosting platform, running the FastAPI backend in Docker containers. Fargate eliminates the need to provision and manage servers, providing a true serverless container experience.

**Configuration Details:**

The backend application runs in containers with 512 CPU units (0.5 vCPU) and 1024 MB memory, providing sufficient resources for typical workloads. The desired task count is set to one for development, with auto-scaling policies ready to increase this based on CPU and memory utilization. Containers are deployed in private subnets for security, with the Application Load Balancer handling all external traffic.

**Justification and Benefits:**

ECS Fargate was selected for several compelling reasons. The serverless nature eliminates server management overhead, including patching, scaling, and monitoring. Automatic scaling responds to traffic changes without manual intervention, ensuring consistent performance during peak periods. The pay-per-use pricing model optimizes costs by charging only for actual container runtime. Task-level isolation ensures security, with each container having dedicated resources and IAM roles. Built-in integration with CloudWatch, Application Load Balancer, and Secrets Manager simplifies operations and monitoring.

**Business Value:**

This service reduces infrastructure management time by approximately 70%, allowing developers to focus on application features rather than server maintenance. The automatic scaling capability supports business growth without requiring infrastructure redesign. High availability is built in, with automatic task replacement if containers become unhealthy.

### 3.2 Amazon Relational Database Service (RDS) - PostgreSQL

**Purpose and Implementation:**

Amazon RDS hosts the PostgreSQL database with pgvector extension, serving as the central data repository for structured data and vector embeddings. The database stores document metadata, query logs, system metrics, and 1536-dimensional vector embeddings for semantic search.

**Configuration Details:**

The development environment uses a db.t3.micro instance with 20 GB GP3 SSD storage that auto-scales to 100 GB as needed. Automated backups run daily with a 7-day retention period. The database is configured with encryption at rest using AWS KMS and encrypted connections for data in transit. Performance Insights provides detailed monitoring of database performance and query patterns.

**Justification and Benefits:**

RDS was chosen over self-managed databases for several critical advantages. AWS handles all database administration tasks including backups, patching, and monitoring. The managed service guarantees 99.95% availability with automated failover in Multi-AZ configurations. Point-in-time recovery enables restoration to any point within the backup retention period. The pgvector extension eliminates the need for a separate vector database, simplifying architecture while providing efficient vector similarity search. Native SQL integration allows complex queries combining relational and vector operations.

**Business Value:**

The managed database service reduces database administration costs by 80% compared to self-managed solutions. Automated backups and point-in-time recovery ensure data protection without manual intervention. The unified database simplifies application logic and reduces integration complexity. Performance monitoring and optimization features maintain optimal database performance as data volume grows.

### 3.3 Amazon Simple Storage Service (S3)

**Purpose and Implementation:**

Amazon S3 provides highly durable, scalable storage for all uploaded policy documents. The system creates unique keys for each document and maintains version history for audit and compliance purposes.

**Configuration Details:**

Documents are stored in a dedicated S3 bucket with server-side encryption using AES-256. Versioning is enabled to maintain historical versions of all documents. Bucket policies enforce strict access controls, allowing access only from ECS task roles. Lifecycle policies automatically transition objects through storage classes: Standard for active use, Standard-IA after 30 days, and Glacier Deep Archive after 90 days.

**Justification and Benefits:**

S3 was selected as the document storage solution due to its exceptional durability (99.999999999%), unlimited scalability, extremely low cost, seamless integration with other AWS services, and support for sophisticated lifecycle management. Unlike file systems that require capacity planning, S3 automatically scales to accommodate any volume of documents. The service-level agreement guarantees 99.9% availability, ensuring documents are accessible when needed.

**Business Value:**

Lifecycle policies reduce storage costs by 70-80% by automatically moving older documents to cheaper storage tiers. The pay-per-use model eliminates upfront capacity planning and over-provisioning. Versioning provides complete audit trails for regulatory compliance. Integration with CloudWatch enables monitoring of storage usage and access patterns for capacity planning and security analysis.

### 3.4 Application Load Balancer (ALB)

**Purpose and Implementation:**

The Application Load Balancer distributes incoming HTTP/HTTPS traffic across ECS containers, providing high availability and automatic failover. It serves as the single entry point for all client requests, implementing health checks and SSL termination.

**Configuration Details:**

The ALB is deployed in public subnets across two availability zones for redundancy. It listens on ports 80 (HTTP) and 443 (HTTPS), with HTTP traffic optionally redirected to HTTPS for security. Health checks query the application's health endpoint every 30 seconds, automatically removing unhealthy containers from rotation. The target group forwards traffic to ECS tasks on port 8000 using the awsvpc network mode.

**Justification and Benefits:**

ALB was chosen over other load balancing options for its advanced routing capabilities, native ECS integration, WebSocket support, and cost-effectiveness for HTTP/HTTPS traffic. The Layer 7 load balancing enables sophisticated routing based on URL paths, headers, and query parameters. SSL termination offloads encryption overhead from application containers, improving performance and simplifying certificate management.

**Business Value:**

The load balancer provides high availability by distributing traffic across multiple containers and automatically failing over to healthy instances. SSL termination simplifies certificate management with centralized SSL configuration. Health monitoring ensures only healthy containers receive traffic, maintaining service quality. Integration with AWS Certificate Manager enables free SSL certificates, reducing costs compared to commercial certificate authorities.

### 3.5 Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)

**Purpose and Implementation:**

The VPC provides an isolated network environment for all IntelliClaim resources, enabling complete control over network topology, routing, and security. The network is designed with security and high availability as primary considerations.

**Configuration Details:**

The VPC uses the 10.0.0.0/16 CIDR block, providing 65,536 IP addresses for future growth. Two public subnets (10.0.0.0/24 and 10.0.1.0/24) host internet-facing resources like the load balancer. Two private subnets (10.0.2.0/24 and 10.0.3.0/24) host application containers and the database. An Internet Gateway enables public subnet internet access, while a NAT Gateway provides outbound internet access for private subnet resources. VPC Endpoints for S3 and ECR eliminate internet traffic for AWS service access, reducing costs and improving security.

**Justification and Benefits:**

VPC provides network isolation, ensuring IntelliClaim resources are completely separated from other AWS customers and services. Security groups and network ACLs implement multiple layers of network security. The multi-AZ design across two availability zones ensures high availability even if one zone becomes unavailable. VPC Flow Logs capture all network traffic for security analysis and troubleshooting.

**Business Value:**

Network segmentation isolates critical components like databases from direct internet access, significantly reducing attack surface. VPC Endpoints reduce data transfer costs by keeping AWS service traffic within the AWS network. The flexible network design supports future growth, including additional application tiers, microservices, and multi-region deployments. Compliance with industry security standards is facilitated by the comprehensive network security controls.

### 3.6 AWS Secrets Manager

**Purpose and Implementation:**

Secrets Manager securely stores and manages sensitive information including API keys, database credentials, and encryption keys. The service provides automatic rotation capabilities and integrates seamlessly with ECS for injection into containers at runtime.

**Configuration Details:**

Two secrets are created: application secrets containing AIMLAPI and OpenAI API keys, and database credentials containing RDS username and password. Secrets are encrypted at rest using AWS KMS. ECS task definitions reference secrets by ARN, with AWS automatically injecting values as environment variables at container startup. CloudWatch Logs capture all secret access for audit purposes.

**Justification and Benefits:**

Secrets Manager eliminates the security risks of storing credentials in code, configuration files, or environment variables. Centralized secret management provides a single source of truth for credentials across all environments. Automatic rotation capabilities enable regular credential updates without application downtime. Fine-grained access control through IAM ensures only authorized services can access secrets. Audit logging provides complete visibility into secret access patterns.

**Business Value:**

Eliminating hardcoded credentials significantly reduces security risk and simplifies credential management across multiple environments. Automatic rotation satisfies compliance requirements for regular credential updates. Integration with ECS eliminates manual secret distribution, reducing operational overhead. The service costs only $0.40 per secret per month, providing exceptional value for the security benefits.

### 3.7 AWS Key Management Service (KMS)

**Purpose and Implementation:**

KMS manages encryption keys for data at rest across all AWS services. Customer-managed keys provide control over encryption and meet compliance requirements for key management.

**Configuration Details:**

A customer-managed key encrypts RDS database storage, ensuring all data including backups is protected. Automatic key rotation is enabled, creating new cryptographic material annually while maintaining access to data encrypted with old keys. Key policies restrict key usage to authorized IAM roles, preventing unauthorized decryption. CloudWatch Logs capture all key usage for compliance auditing.

**Justification and Benefits:**

Customer-managed keys provide greater control compared to AWS-managed keys, enabling key policies, audit trails, and custom rotation schedules. Integration with other AWS services ensures consistent encryption across the entire infrastructure. The service provides FIPS 140-2 validated hardware security modules for key generation and protection. Key deletion requires a 7-30 day waiting period, protecting against accidental deletion.

**Business Value:**

Encryption at rest satisfies regulatory requirements for healthcare and financial data protection. Centralized key management simplifies compliance auditing and key lifecycle management. The ability to disable keys provides an emergency mechanism to prevent data access if needed. CloudWatch integration enables monitoring of key usage patterns for security analysis.

### 3.8 Amazon CloudWatch

**Purpose and Implementation:**

CloudWatch provides comprehensive monitoring, logging, and alerting for all infrastructure and application components. The service aggregates metrics from multiple sources into a unified monitoring platform.

**Configuration Details:**

Log Groups collect application logs from ECS containers, VPC flow logs for network traffic, and RDS logs for database activities. Metrics track ECS container CPU and memory utilization, RDS database connections and query performance, ALB request counts and latencies, and S3 storage usage. Alarms trigger notifications when metrics exceed thresholds, such as high CPU utilization or elevated error rates. Dashboards visualize key metrics in real-time, providing operational visibility.

**Justification and Benefits:**

CloudWatch's native integration with all AWS services provides automatic metric collection without additional configuration. Centralized logging simplifies troubleshooting by aggregating logs from distributed components. Custom metrics enable application-specific monitoring beyond infrastructure metrics. Alarm actions can trigger automatic remediation through Lambda functions or SNS notifications. Log retention policies balance storage costs with audit requirements.

**Business Value:**

Unified monitoring reduces mean time to detection for issues, enabling faster resolution. Automated alerts ensure the operations team is notified immediately when problems occur. Historical metrics support capacity planning and performance optimization. The service provides valuable insights into application usage patterns, informing business decisions. CloudWatch Insights enables sophisticated log analysis without expensive third-party tools.

### 3.9 AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM)

**Purpose and Implementation:**

IAM implements authentication and authorization for all AWS resource access. Fine-grained policies ensure each component has only the permissions necessary for its function, following the principle of least privilege.

**Configuration Details:**

The ECS Execution Role grants permissions to pull container images from ECR, push logs to CloudWatch, and retrieve secrets from Secrets Manager. The ECS Task Role provides permissions for application containers to access S3 buckets, connect to RDS, and write CloudWatch metrics. The RDS Monitoring Role enables enhanced monitoring metrics. Service roles for VPC Flow Logs, CloudWatch, and other services follow similar least-privilege patterns.

**Justification and Benefits:**

Role-based access control eliminates long-term credentials, reducing security risk. Temporary security credentials issued to roles automatically rotate, preventing credential compromise. Policy conditions enable sophisticated access control based on time, source IP, and resource tags. Cross-service integration uses IAM roles, avoiding credential management complexity. CloudTrail logs all IAM activities for security auditing.

**Business Value:**

The zero-trust security model implemented through IAM reduces the risk of unauthorized access. Granular permissions ensure compromised components cannot access unrelated resources. Regular access reviews identify and remove unnecessary permissions, maintaining security posture over time. IAM Access Analyzer identifies publicly accessible resources, preventing accidental data exposure.

### 3.10 Amazon Elastic Container Registry (ECR)

**Purpose and Implementation:**

ECR stores Docker container images for the backend application. Integration with ECS enables automatic image deployment to Fargate tasks.

**Configuration Details:**

A private repository stores the intelliclaim-backend image with automatic image scanning for vulnerabilities. Lifecycle policies automatically delete untagged images after 14 days and retain only the 10 most recent tagged images. Encryption at rest protects image data. IAM policies restrict repository access to authorized roles.

**Justification and Benefits:**

ECR's native integration with ECS simplifies container deployment compared to third-party registries. Automatic vulnerability scanning identifies security issues before deployment. The fully managed service eliminates the operational overhead of running a private registry. Transfer between ECR and ECS within the same region incurs no data transfer costs. Image signing verifies image authenticity and integrity.

**Business Value:**

Eliminating third-party registry costs and complexity reduces operational expenses. Vulnerability scanning prevents deployment of images with known security issues. Private repositories ensure intellectual property protection. Integration with ECS enables rapid deployment and rollback of application updates.

### 3.11 Security Groups

**Purpose and Implementation:**

Security Groups implement stateful network firewalls for AWS resources, controlling inbound and outbound traffic at the network interface level.

**Configuration Details:**

The ALB Security Group allows HTTP (80) and HTTPS (443) from the internet, enabling client access. The ECS Tasks Security Group allows traffic on port 8000 only from the ALB security group, preventing direct access from the internet. The RDS Security Group allows PostgreSQL traffic (5432) only from the ECS Tasks security group, isolating the database. VPC Endpoint Security Groups allow HTTPS (443) from the VPC CIDR, securing AWS service access.

**Justification and Benefits:**

Security Group rules are stateful, automatically allowing response traffic without explicit outbound rules. Reference-based rules using security group IDs create dynamic relationships that adapt as resources are added or removed. Default deny ensures only explicitly allowed traffic is permitted. Multiple security groups can be attached to resources, enabling layered security policies.

**Business Value:**

Implementing least-privilege network access significantly reduces attack surface. Isolating the database from internet access prevents common attack vectors. The layered security approach provides defense in depth, ensuring security even if one layer is compromised. Security group rules can be updated without resource restarts, enabling rapid security response.

